- No filler: every clause carries information, because a sentence that works for its keep tells the reader more than one that fills. No sentence exists only to announce, preview, praise or summarise; no trailing clause explains why a sentence was written; no sentence stacks synonyms; no vague intensifiers pad a point. A list lead-in states the split plainly (who splits what, into how many parts) and nothing about its purpose.
- Keywords: a keyword is a topic, not a fixed string, because a sentence is read by a person, not by a machine. Rephrase a keyword so the sentence reads naturally: change the word order, inflect its words, and put a small word of your own between the parts. A keyword that cannot sit naturally is covered by meaning; never force it into a noun pile.
- Plain wording: the reader has no specialist vocabulary, so a technical term is defined in plain words the first time it is used, and a sentence that works without it leaves it out. No jargon the ordinary reader would not know, and no hype words.
