INFO:revamp:[write] RAW RESPONSE:
{
  "intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Software can now pull readable text off websites and hand it to artificial intelligence systems, which use that text to learn and answer questions. Anyone weighing that task should know about the wider business behind it. Collecting information from websites by automated means has become a routine business activity rather than an occasional one-off job. Companies gather public web pages to study competitors, track prices, follow customer reviews and understand what shoppers want. Much of this work now runs through rented online services, so companies do not have to own and maintain the equipment themselves. That wider business is the [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]].",
      "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]"
    ]
  },
  "transition": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Pulling readable text from websites is one slice of a wider paid market for web data. In that market, companies buy a service that visits websites and returns the information they need, and they buy it because gathering that information themselves takes time and equipment they would rather not run. The buyers range from small research teams to large retailers, and they all want the same thing: reliable data without the upkeep.",
      "The sections below set out how large this market is and where it is heading."
    ]
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "sop": "report_context",
      "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The web scraping services market was worth USD 480 million in 2025, according to intelmarketresearch.com. That figure covers the fees companies pay for automated collection of web data. The same research puts the market at USD 910 million by 2034, which works out to growth of 7.3% a year between 2026 and 2034. The size of the web scraping services market matters because it shows how much companies already spend on rented data collection rather than on their own equipment. Steady growth at that pace points to a business that is expanding without racing ahead of its customers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "trends",
      "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Three forces are reshaping the market: AI-assisted extraction is spreading but not yet standard, anti-bot defences are getting harder to beat, and website owners are blocking bots and pushing back legally. The trends now shaping the web scraping services market pull in different directions, and each one changes how providers win work.\n- AI-assisted extraction is spreading but not yet the norm. Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey, published in January 2026, found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. Among the same respondents, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted tools, while 33.8% said they do not plan to use them even in the future.\n- Anti-bot systems are getting harder to beat. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that defences have evolved from simple rule-based blocking into multi-layered systems powered by behavioural analysis, fingerprinting and machine learning. That shift has made scraping more complex, less predictable and more resource intensive, which pushes enterprises to rethink how they design and run data collection.\n- Website owners are blocking bots and pushing back legally. A growing number of sites put technical barriers in place during 2026 to keep AI agents off their content, and publishers cited scraping that drains server resources without credit or payment. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that objecting site owners may now invoke anti-circumvention laws, codified in the U.S. as 17 U.S.C., alongside breach of contract, copyright and computer hacking theories."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "segmentation",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The report splits the web scraping services market into three parts: delivery type, application and region.\n- By delivery type, the categories are browser extension, installable software and cloud based.\n- By application, the categories are data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other uses.\n- By region, the categories are North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "subsegment",
      "heading": "Sub-Segment Analysis of Web Scraping Services",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "segment": "type",
          "sub_segment": "Cloud Based",
          "paragraph": "Cloud-based delivery is the category to watch among the delivery types. Rented online services remove the need for a company to own and maintain its own collection equipment, and that is the main reason buyers choose them. Published pricing shows how the model works. AIJourn compared plans in October 2026 and found Firecrawl's Standard plan at $83 per month for 100,000 credits and ZenRows' Launch plan at $58 for 250,000 credits. The comparison also noted that one page costs one credit, that JSON extraction adds four credits, and that pages returning 403 or 404 still consume one credit. ScrapeBadger's pay-as-you-go rate came in at $0.15 per 1,000 credits, against $0.83 per 1,000 credits on Firecrawl Standard. Those prices put cloud delivery within reach of small teams, and the opportunities in the web scraping services market sit largely in that low entry cost."
        },
        {
          "segment": "application",
          "sub_segment": "Market Research",
          "paragraph": "Market research is the application that turns collected pages into decisions. Teams use it to watch competitors, follow prices and read customer reviews, and the output feeds straight into planning. How large web scraping for market research has become is hard to pin down from the categories alone, but the application sits alongside data aggregation and customer insight as one of the main uses of the web scraping services market. Research use adds value to the market because it turns raw pages into findings a business can act on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "country_us",
      "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "North America led the market in 2018, when it held a major share on the strength of a large number of key players based there. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest-growing region. The web scraping services market share by region still leans toward North America, and that matters for buyers because a crowded home market tends to mean more providers to choose from and more competition on price. Asia Pacific tells a different story, with faster growth expected as more companies in the region take up rented data collection. Website owners are also tightening access to their pages. Cloudflare announced that from September 15, 2026, its default settings will block mixed-use crawlers from any page that hosts ads, separating crawlers used for traditional search from those used for AI agents and training. Meta removed a controversial scraping clause from its commercial terms of use on October 17, 2026, after legal experts and the public pushed back."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "key_players",
      "heading": "Key Players in the Web Scraping Services Market",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market is crowded, and the report names Phantombuster, PilotFish, Mozenda, Diggernaut, Datahut and UiPath among the participants. The field is highly fragmented across proxy aggregators and managed extraction platforms, so no single company dominates.",
        "Companies that offer web scraping services range from small specialist tools to large automation vendors. The report also lists Kuaiyi Technology, SysNucleus, Parseur, Octopus Data and Salestools.io among the players."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "conclusion",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Outlook",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The outlook for the web scraping services market points to steady growth rather than a surge. The forecast above takes the market from its 2025 level to USD 910 million by 2034, a modest pace spread over nine years. Demand from digital marketing keeps that growth coming, as companies want competitor prices, customer reviews and shopper preferences without building their own collection systems. Piracy and tighter controls on website access will shape how services are delivered, pushing providers toward managed environments and careful compliance. A CMI analyst expects the market to keep growing at that modest pace, with providers competing more on reliability and legal care than on raw speed."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
