ervice.\n- By application, the uses are data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other work.\n- By region, the market covers North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."]}, {"sop": "subsegment", "heading": "Sub-Segment Analysis of the Web Scraping Services Market", "paragraphs": [{"segment": "type", "sub_segment": "Cloud Based", "paragraph": "Cloud based delivery carries much of the pull toward rented collection. A buyer runs the work on someone else's servers and pays by usage, so no equipment sits in-house. AIJourn compared published prices in October 2026 and found Firecrawl's Standard plan at USD 83 a month for 100,000 credits, against ZenRows' Launch plan at USD 58 for 250,000 credits. The comparison notes that one page costs one credit, that JSON extraction adds four credits, and that pages returning an error still consume one. Pricing like this shapes how buyers weigh the web scraping services market, because cost follows volume rather than a fixed fee."}, {"segment": "application", "sub_segment": "Market Research", "paragraph": "Market research use gives the market a steady base of demand. Companies want competitor pages, price changes and customer reviews gathered on a schedule, and they want the output ready for analysis. That need fits the move toward managed, AI-ready collection, since clean text can go straight into a model. Market research data of this kind is the reason many buyers start with a service rather than building their own stack."}]}, {"sop": "country_us", "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["North America held a major share of the web scraping services market in 2018, and the region's many key players explain that lead. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region. A look at web scraping services market share by region shows why buyers watch both: North America offers a deep bench of providers, while Asia Pacific offers the faster pace of growth. The United States sits at the center of the legal debate over access. Meta removed a controversial scraping clause from its commercial terms on October 17, 2026, after legal experts and the public pushed back, TechJuice reported. The original clause would have let Meta use content from business accounts to train and improve its products. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners who object to scraping can now invoke anti-circumvention laws, codified in the US as 17 U.S.C., alongside breach of contract, copyright and computer hacking claims. Many earlier challenges to scraping public sites failed, so these technical protection measures give objecting owners a newer set of arguments."]}, {"sop": "key_players", "heading": "Companies that lead the web scraping services market", "paragraphs": ["The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market looks crowded rather than settled, and the report names Phantombuster, UiPath, Mozenda, Parseur, PilotFish and Diggernaut among the players. The field stretches across automation platforms, extraction tools and software suites, with no single firm holding the ground. Companies that offer web scraping services range from small specialist tools to large automation vendors, and the report also lists Datahut, Kuaiyi Technology, SysNucleus, Octopus Data and Salestools.io."]}, {"sop": "conclusion", "heading": "Conclusion and Outlook for the Web Scraping Services Market", "paragraphs": ["The web scraping services market is growing at a modest but steady pace, and the forecast above points to a market roughly twice its present size within a decade. Three forces will shape that path. AI-assisted extraction is spreading without yet being universal, anti-bot defenses keep getting harder to beat, and site owners are blocking bots more firmly. Cloud delivery and market research use carry much of the demand, and a crowded field of vendors keeps pressure on price. The outlook for the web scraping services market therefore favors managed, AI-ready collection even as access to web content gets harder. A CMI analyst notes that vendors who solve access and compliance will likely win more business than those who compete on price alone."]}]}
INFO:revamp:[write] PARSED JSON:
{
  "intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Software that reads text off websites has become a working tool for many businesses. The idea is simple: a program visits a public web page, copies the words and numbers on it, and hands them back in a clean form. That clean text then feeds an artificial intelligence system, which can study it, sort it or answer questions from it. Anyone weighing this task should know that it sits inside a much larger business, the [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]], where companies pay specialists to gather web data for them. Collecting information from websites by automated means is now routine rather than occasional. Firms pull public pages to study competitors, watch prices, follow reviews and learn what shoppers want. Much of the work runs through rented online services, so a company does not have to own or maintain the equipment itself.",
      "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]",
      "The choice is really about how much of that work a company wants to own, and how much it would rather hand to someone else."
    ]
  },
  "transition": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Pulling readable text from a website is one slice of that larger business. The market for web scraping services covers the tools and the people who collect web data on someone else's behalf. Buyers are companies that need a steady flow of public information but lack the time, staff or equipment to gather it themselves. They pay for a service that runs the collection, keeps it working and delivers the results in a usable form. The sections ahead show how large this business has become and where it is heading."
    ]
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "sop": "report_context",
      "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The web scraping services market was worth USD 480 million in 2025, intelmarketresearch.com reports. That figure measures what buyers spend on services that collect public web data for them. The market is forecast to reach USD 910 million by 2034. Growth is expected to run at a 7.3% compound annual rate over 2026 to 2034, a pace that would roughly double the size of the market across that stretch. The size of the web scraping services market matters because it shows how much companies now budget for web data extraction as a routine expense. Steady growth of this kind points to demand that keeps widening rather than a short burst of interest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "trends",
      "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Three shifts stand out in the trends now shaping the web scraping services market: artificial intelligence is spreading through the work, defenses against bots are getting harder to beat, and website owners are pushing back more firmly.\n- A survey by Apify and The Web Scraping Club, published in January 2026, found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. Among the same respondents, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted tools, so adoption is likely to keep climbing.\n- Browserless reported in January 2026 that the work is moving toward AI-driven, cloud-based and compliance-aware methods, and away from do-it-yourself setups built on page selectors. The firm credits AI-native extraction, tougher anti-bot systems and rising compliance pressure, which push more teams toward real browsers and managed environments.\n- Grepsr reported in April 2026 that anti-bot systems have grown from simple rule-based blocking into layered defenses that lean on behavioral analysis, fingerprinting and machine learning. That shift makes scraping more complex, less predictable and more resource intensive, and it pushes enterprises to rethink how they design and run data collection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "segmentation",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The report splits the web scraping services market into three parts by delivery type, four by application and six by region.\n- By delivery type, buyers choose a browser extension, installable software or a cloud based service.\n- By application, the uses are data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other work.\n- By region, the market covers North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "subsegment",
      "heading": "Sub-Segment Analysis of the Web Scraping Services Market",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "segment": "type",
          "sub_segment": "Cloud Based",
          "paragraph": "Cloud based delivery carries much of the pull toward rented collection. A buyer runs the work on someone else's servers and pays by usage, so no equipment sits in-house. AIJourn compared published prices in October 2026 and found Firecrawl's Standard plan at USD 83 a month for 100,000 credits, against ZenRows' Launch plan at USD 58 for 250,000 credits. The comparison notes that one page costs one credit, that JSON extraction adds four credits, and that pages returning an error still consume one. Pricing like this shapes how buyers weigh the web scraping services market, because cost follows volume rather than a fixed fee."
        },
        {
          "segment": "application",
          "sub_segment": "Market Research",
          "paragraph": "Market research use gives the market a steady base of demand. Companies want competitor pages, price changes and customer reviews gathered on a schedule, and they want the output ready for analysis. That need fits the move toward managed, AI-ready collection, since clean text can go straight into a model. Market research data of this kind is the reason many buyers start with a service rather than building their own stack."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "country_us",
      "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "North America held a major share of the web scraping services market in 2018, and the region's many key players explain that lead. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region. A look at web scraping services market share by region shows why buyers watch both: North America offers a deep bench of providers, while Asia Pacific offers the faster pace of growth. The United States sits at the center of the legal debate over access. Meta removed a controversial scraping clause from its commercial terms on October 17, 2026, after legal experts and the public pushed back, TechJuice reported. The original clause would have let Meta use content from business accounts to train and improve its products. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners who object to scraping can now invoke anti-circumvention laws, codified in the US as 17 U.S.C., alongside breach of contract, copyright and computer hacking claims. Many earlier challenges to scraping public sites failed, so these technical protection measures give objecting owners a newer set of arguments."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "key_players",
      "heading": "Companies that lead the web scraping services market",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market looks crowded rather than settled, and the report names Phantombuster, UiPath, Mozenda, Parseur, PilotFish and Diggernaut among the players. The field stretches across automation platforms, extraction tools and software suites, with no single firm holding the ground. Companies that offer web scraping services range from small specialist tools to large automation vendors, and the report also lists Datahut, Kuaiyi Technology, SysNucleus, Octopus Data and Salestools.io."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "conclusion",
      "heading": "Conclusion and Outlook for the Web Scraping Services Market",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The web scraping services market is growing at a modest but steady pace, and the forecast above points to a market roughly twice its present size within a decade. Three forces will shape that path. AI-assisted extraction is spreading without yet being universal, anti-bot defenses keep getting harder to beat, and site owners are blocking bots more firmly. Cloud delivery and market research use carry much of the demand, and a crowded field of vendors keeps pressure on price. The outlook for the web scraping services market therefore favors managed, AI-ready collection even as access to web content gets harder. A CMI analyst notes that vendors who solve access and compliance will likely win more business than those who compete on price alone."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
