INFO:revamp:[write] RAW RESPONSE:
{"intro": {"paragraphs": ["Software can now pull readable text off websites and hand it to artificial intelligence systems, which use that text to learn and answer questions. Anyone weighing that task soon runs into a bigger question: where the text comes from, and who collects it. Automated collection of public web pages has become routine business work rather than an occasional chore. Companies gather pages to study competitors, track prices, follow customer reviews and learn what shoppers want. Much of the work now runs through rented online services, so a company does not have to own and maintain the equipment itself. That wider business of collecting web data is what the [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]] covers.", "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]"]}, "transition": {"paragraphs": ["Collecting web data is not just a technical chore any more. It is a market of its own, made up of firms that sell the tools and the managed collection that other companies rely on. Buyers range from small teams that need a steady feed of prices to large organisations that want clean, ready-to-use data without running servers of their own. They pay for speed, reliability and someone else carrying the maintenance burden. The size, trends, segments and leaders of that market are what follow."]}, "sections": [{"sop": "report_context", "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["The market for web scraping services was worth USD 480 million in 2025, according to intelmarketresearch.com, and it is growing at a modest but steady pace. That figure measures what buyers spend on tools and services that collect public web data. The same research puts the market at USD 910 million by 2034, roughly double its 2025 value. Growth runs at a compound annual rate of 7.3% across the years from 2026 to 2034. The size of the web scraping services market therefore stays small next to many software categories, yet it keeps climbing as more companies treat web data extraction as a standing need rather than a one-off project."]}, {"sop": "trends", "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?", "paragraphs": ["Three forces are reshaping how collection gets done, and each one pushes buyers toward more managed, more careful setups.\n- AI-assisted extraction is spreading, but it is not yet the norm. Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey, published in January 2026, found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. Among those surveyed, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted tools, and 33.8% said they have no such plans. These trends in the web scraping services market point to steady adoption rather than a sudden switch.\n- Anti-bot defenses are getting harder to beat. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that blocking systems have moved from simple rules to layered defenses that study behavior, fingerprint a browser and lean on machine learning. Collection has become more complex, less predictable and more resource intensive as a result.\n- Websites are pushing back, both technically and legally. Browserless reported in January 2026 that tougher anti-bot systems and rising compliance pressure are pushing teams toward real browsers and managed environments. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners can now invoke anti-circumvention laws, alongside contract, copyright and computer hacking claims, against collectors they object to."]}, {"sop": "segmentation", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation", "paragraphs": ["The CMI report splits the market into parts along three lines. The segmentation of the web scraping services market covers delivery type, application and region.\n- By delivery type, buyers choose between a browser extension, installable software and cloud based tools.\n- By application, the work serves data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other uses.\n- By region, the market spans North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."]}, {"sop": "subsegment", "heading": "Sub-Segment Analysis of the Web Scraping Services Market", "paragraphs": [{"segment": "type", "sub_segment": "Cloud Based", "paragraph": "Cloud based tools stand out among the delivery types because they remove the need to own and maintain equipment. A team rents capacity instead, and the provider handles the servers, the scheduling and the upkeep. AIJourn compared published prices for scraping tools in October 2026 and found Firecrawl's Standard plan at USD 83 a month for 100,000 credits, against ZenRows' Launch plan at USD 58 for 250,000 credits. The comparison also noted that a page costs one credit, that structured extraction adds four more, and that failed pages still use a credit. Pricing like this shapes the opportunities in the web scraping services market, because buyers can start small and scale as their needs grow."}, {"segment": "application", "sub_segment": "Market Research", "paragraph": "Market research is one of the applications the market is split by, and it covers the work of watching competitors, prices and customer sentiment at scale. Anyone asking how large web scraping for market research has become is really asking how much of the market this work accounts for, and the answer sits inside the application split rather than beside it. The work suits rented tools well, since a research team needs fresh pages on a schedule without building its own collection system. Market research adds steady, repeat demand to the web scraping services market, and that demand tends to continue rather than stop after a single project."}]}, {"sop": "country_us", "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["North America led the market in 2018, when a large number of key players gave the region a major share, according to the CMI report. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region. The web scraping services market share by region follows that pattern, with a mature leader and a faster-moving challenger.\n- Cloudflare said in June 2026 that from September 15, 2026 its default settings would block mixed-use crawlers from any page that hosts ads, separating search crawlers from those used for AI agents.\n- Meta removed a disputed scraping clause from its commercial terms on October 17, 2026, after legal experts and the public pushed back.\nFor buyers, the picture means the established region still holds the deepest pool of providers, while growth shifts toward Asia Pacific."]}, {"sop": "key_players", "heading": "Key Players in the Web Scraping Services Market", "paragraphs": ["The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market is fragmented, with no single company dominating. The CMI report names Phantombuster, PilotFish, Mozenda, Diggernaut, Datahut and UiPath among the players, and the full list runs longer still, taking in Kuaiyi Technology, SysNucleus, Parseur, Octopus Data and Salestools.io. The field stretches from automation platforms to data-extraction tools, so buyers meet a wide range of companies that offer web scraping services. That spread gives buyers plenty of choice, and it keeps any one vendor from setting the terms for everyone."]}, {"sop": "conclusion", "heading": "Conclusion and Outlook for the Web Scraping Services Market", "paragraphs": ["The market is set to keep growing toward the forecast value, roughly doubling over the decade, at a modest pace rather than a surge. Collection is shifting toward managed, AI-ready services, where providers handle the servers, the scheduling and the upkeep. Demand is pulled along by digital marketing data, as teams watch competitors, prices and reviews on a schedule. Software piracy holds the market back, because copied tools undercut the vendors that build and maintain them. The outlook for the web scraping services market therefore rests on steady, unspectacular gains. A CMI analyst expects the providers that pair managed collection with AI-ready output to take share from those that sell bare tools."]}]}
