      "sop": "trends",
      "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Three forces are reshaping how collection gets done, and each one pushes buyers toward more managed, more careful setups.\n- AI-assisted extraction is spreading, but it is not yet the norm. Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey, published in January 2026, found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. Among those surveyed, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted tools, and 33.8% said they have no such plans. These trends in the web scraping services market point to steady adoption rather than a sudden switch.\n- Anti-bot defenses are getting harder to beat. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that blocking systems have moved from simple rules to layered defenses that study behavior, fingerprint a browser and lean on machine learning. Collection has become more complex, less predictable and more resource intensive as a result.\n- Websites are pushing back, both technically and legally. Browserless reported in January 2026 that tougher anti-bot systems and rising compliance pressure are pushing teams toward real browsers and managed environments. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners can now invoke anti-circumvention laws, alongside contract, copyright and computer hacking claims, against collectors they object to."
