        "Growth comes from firms moving their services online, from automated advice tools and from clients who expect more from an app. Several forces push adoption at once, and a few obstacles slow it down.\n- Rapid digitization and a surge in online trading are the main drivers of the wealth management platform market, as firms race to put advice and trading on the web.\n- Robo-advisory services and virtual financial assistants let clients manage their money with less hand-holding, which raises revenue for the firms that offer them.\n- Competition among wealth management firms pushes them toward versatile platforms, and younger clients' changing expectations push them further.\nThe high investment costs of wealth management platforms hold some firms back, and a lack of customized products limits what buyers can offer. AI use is spreading quickly: Wealth Management.com reported in October 2026 that 63% of registered investment advisors now use AI, double the share in 2024, yet only one in ten firms has made it a fully integrated part of its strategy."
